{
  "requirement": "`ValidationCache::new()` creates an empty cache for running criteria without\nan agent.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<D: Domain> ValidationCache<D> {\n    pub fn new() -> Self;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.validation-cache.new` has one obligation. `ValidationCache::new()` must create an empty cache that runs criteria without an agent.\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[new_cache_starts_without_files_or_agent]` calls `ValidationCache::<TestDomain>::new()`. It asserts `agent.is_none()`, which covers \"without an agent\". It also asserts `sources.is_none()` and that the `outlines` map is empty, which covers \"empty\" for the file and source state.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_clones_share_the_first_successful_outline_for_each_path]` is marked as a test of this requirement. It builds a cache with `new()` and shows it starts with no cached outline for a path. The first `from_path` on the missing file fails, and a later read caches the first successful outline. The test then uses the cache without an agent.\n\nThe struct also has internal bookkeeping fields: `usage`, `nodes` and `source_addresses`. The unit test doesn't assert that these are empty. `nodes` is private and the other two are bookkeeping. `new()` has no condition that could pre-populate them, and the text names no behavior for them beyond \"empty\". I don't count this as a gap.",
  "references": "bb6fff83f12d9662bcbd1e2eb6088fd8333e94ff01a7055d64c95abde7af5e69",
